From 246c26f5ca9cdfedc17fb4c48b9dbb2d8507849e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 03:45:20 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] shared blockers: a park probed on another gateway
 keeps its own wait when the wall clears

Problem: on the MacBook board (home of the cards), 2026-11-15 PST, the release lane woke two landers out of
their exact-head CI waits: t_39b8e8a25149df65 parked at 02:40:41 and was woken at 02:40:44; t_4aeee1f1b1fef2a1
parked at 03:06:43 and was woken at 03:07:10. Both events were `shared_blocker_cleared {"cleared_via": "check",
"woke": true, "status": "ready"}` for `github-daveey-core-reserve`. Each woken card could only re-park, which
costs one paced worker run per card per clear.

Why 1: release_cleared_blockers took both for bare clock parks. own_wait was False, because its branch writes
       no `woke`.
Why 2: own_wait (c18824850e) needs `kwc.get_state(conn, tid) is not None`. The home had no task_wake_checks row
       for either card (board read 03:4x PST). The park payloads say `probe_host: worker-7` and `worker-8`.
Why 3: both parks ran on pool workers. park_fields stamps probe_host as the parking gateway, and set_check
       registers the probe only there (kanban_timed_park.py `register_here=kwc.probe_here(...)`). That gateway runs
       the probe and relays the wake home. This is by design.
Root (happened): the independent-park rule keys "has its own probe" on the local execution row, not on the
       logical probe the park recorded (wake_check + probe_host). So every park probed elsewhere looks
       probe-less on its home.
Branch (c): when such a card's own probe exits 0 while the wall is still open, the guard holds it until the probe
       interval. A re-park without the id keeps the card in `behind` by design, and nothing tells "ready because
       its own wait finished" from "ready behind the wall". Before this change test (c) fails with
       "still held by shared blocker ...: probe interval due at ...".
Root (not caught): every independent-park test parks on the board that runs the probe. No fixture models a
       synced board whose park names another gateway as probe_host.
Recurrence: the 2026-10-28 wake loop (t_7ebc5d5f838cf87c, fixed by _already_saw_clear measuring from the first
       clear) and c18824850e (2026-11-14, own_wait) each covered a different case. Neither covers a remotely
       probed park made by a run that started before the clear.

Change (t_416253bd19b22598, replanned):
- own_wait also counts a park whose wake_check runs on another gateway
  (kanban_blocker_own_wait.probe_elsewhere: the newest park has a wake_check and probe_here() is False).
- _shared_blocker_hold does not hold a card whose newest wake is its own probe's condition_met, when the park
  that set the probe came after the card hit the wall and named neither the wall's id nor awaiting.shared_blocker.
  One run spends it: a `claimed` event after the wake.
- blocker_states is unchanged. The card's first plan (a pass run with a wake_check park sets current = None in
  the walk) would break acceptance 2: test_repark_without_blocker_id_keeps_blocker_open asserts
  set(st.behind) == {a, b} after exactly that worker re-park. kanban_schedule's description also documents that
  a park without blocker_id stays behind the blocker.

Tests (tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_blocker_remote_probe_park.py; the synced board is modelled with own_gateway
patched, the park made as worker-8, and no home probe row):
- (a) remote-probe park survives a registered-check clear: RED on c79f377aef (woken), GREEN after.
- (b) a bare clock re-park from a worker is still woken: GREEN before and after (pin).
- local-probe own wait unchanged (c18824850e case): GREEN before and after (pin).
- (c) its own probe fires while the wall is open, so it is not held (park made on the home, and on the worker):
  RED before, GREEN after.
- pins, GREEN before and after: one run spends the own-wake exemption; a park whose awaiting names the wall
  stays held.
- Every test file importing the shared-blocker modules (33 files, 724 tests) passes on the Mac.
